'''''Nepenthes'' × ''ventrata''''' ({{IPAc-en|n|ᵻ|ˈ|p|ɛ|n|θ|iː|z|_|v|ɛ|n|ˈ|t|r|ɑː|t|ə}}) is a [[natural hybrid]] involving ''[[Nepenthes graciliflora |N. graciliflora ]]''<ref>''Nepenthes graciliflora'' was once known as ''alata'' "Green" but has been considered a separate species since 2013.</ref> and ''[[Nepenthes ventricosa|N. ventricosa]]''. Like its two parent species, it is [[endemic (ecology)|endemic]] to the [[Philippines]]. The name was originally published in the ''[[Carnivorous Plant Newsletter]]'' in 1979.<ref>Fleming, R. 1979. {{cite web|url= http://carnivorousplants.org/cpn/articles/CPNv08n1p10_12.pdf |title=Hybrid ''Nepenthes''. }}&nbsp;{{small|(626&nbsp;[[Kibibyte|KiB]])}} ''[[Carnivorous Plant Newsletter]]'' '''8'''(1): 10–12.</ref> ''Nepenthes'' × ''ventrata'' is one of the most common [[tissue culture]]d ''Nepenthes'' plants, although it is often mislabelled as ''[[Nepenthes alata]]''.<ref>Catalano, M. 2009. ''Nepenthes''. In: ''Growing Carnivores — an Italian perspective''. Prague. pp.&nbsp;50–57.</ref> It is relatively easy to grow indoors and is usually the first tropical [[pitcher plant]] seen by consumers due to its availability in many garden shops and home centres.

The [[cultivar]] [[Nepenthes 'LeeAnn Marie'|''N.'' 'LeeAnn Marie']] is a [[later synonym]] of ''N.''&nbsp;×&nbsp;''ventrata'', although the name is not established as it was not validly published.<ref>Schlauer, J. N.d. [http://www.carnivorousplants.org/cpdb/CPdbDetail.php?cp_id=118907 ''Nepenthes'' 'LeeAnn Marie'] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100925010605/http://carnivorousplants.org/cpdb/CPdbDetail.php?cp_id=118907 |date=2010-09-25 }}. Carnivorous Plant Database.</ref>

